    static string FmtClose(Rt rt, long peer) {
        var ev = new System.Collections.Generic.List<string>();
        for (int i = 0; i < rt.EvKind.Count; i++) ev.Add(rt.EvKind[i] + ":" + rt.EvId[i]);
        var wk = new System.Collections.Generic.List<string>();
        foreach (long w in rt.Woken) wk.Add(w.ToString());
        long bv = rt.Slot(rt.SchedV, Conc.SC_BRIDGES);
        int bn = rt.Objs[(int) bv].Length;
        return (ev.Count == 0 ? "-" : string.Join(",", ev)) + " " + bn + " "
             + (wk.Count == 0 ? "-" : string.Join(",", wk)) + " "
             + (peer == Val.Nil ? "-" : rt.Slot(peer, Conc.PT_STATE).ToString());
    }
--expect
0 2 2 0 1 1 10 3 11 5 11 1 1 5 20 20 2 20 20 2 1 2 10 1 3:30,5:30 0 30,31 4 - 0 40,41 4 3:50,5:50 0 50 2 3:60,5:60 0 60 -
--expect-why
DERIVED FROM THE CONTRACT, not recorded from a run. Four cases, in the order
the driver prints them.

1. NOTHING COLLECTED -- `0 2 2 0`. Both scans answer false, so both functions
   return before allocating: `0` allocations. Both lists are untouched, so
   `2` and `2`. Nothing died, so `0` events.

   THIS IS THE CASE THE WHOLE PORT EXISTS FOR, and the only reading that can
   fail it is `allocs`. The shape this replaced rebuilt both lists on every
   drive iteration whether or not anything had died, and it would answer
   `2 2 0` for the other three readings exactly as this does. That waste was
   ~42 of the ~47 steps a resume cost, billed to the program on the native
   runtime and free on the other two -- so the counter is not belt and
   braces, it is the assertion.

2. ONE BRIDGE COLLECTED -- `1 1 10 3 11 5 11`. ONE allocation and no more:
   the conj for the single survivor. The fresh vector itself is free --
   `empty-vec` hands back a shared singleton -- so the count is the number of
   SURVIVORS, which is the useful thing for it to be. The dead id is NOT
   conj'd, so the list is `1` long, and the survivor kept is `10`. The host
   must hear the pair an explicit close would have produced, in that order
   and for that id: `EV_CLOSED`(3) `11`, then `EV_RELEASE`(5) `11`. Naming
   both the kind AND the id is what stops a transposed or misattributed
   event from passing.

3. A CHANNEL END COLLECTED, PEER OPEN -- `1 1 5 20 20`. Same one
   allocation, same one survivor. The peer's state becomes `P_ORPHANED`(5)
   and not `P_CLOSED`(2): a peer that vanished is not a peer that said
   goodbye, and a host that cannot tell them apart cannot report the
   difference. The waiter on the peer is failed (`20`) and the peer is woken
   (`20`) -- both, because failing a waiter that is never woken is a hang
   dressed as an error.

4. THE SAME, PEER ALREADY CLOSED -- `2 20 20`. The state stays `P_CLOSED`(2).
   An orphaning must not overwrite a close, or a port that was tidily shut
   would start reporting that its peer vanished. It is still failed and
   still woken, because a closed port can still have someone parked on it.

WHAT FAILS IF THE LOGIC IS WRONG -- RUN, not asserted. Six mutations of
`kin/reapports.kin`, each the plausible way to get this wrong, every one
caught on all three targets:

    drop the `any-collected` guard    case 1 allocs   0 -> 2
    conj the dead id too              case 2 count    1 -> 2
    emit EV_CLOSED without EV_RELEASE case 2 has no second event -- crash
    mark the peer CLOSED not ORPHANED case 3 state    5 -> 2
    write the state unconditionally   case 4 state    2 -> 5
    fail the peer but never wake it   case 3 wakes nobody -- crash

The first is why this fixture reads `allocs` at all. Its answer differs from
the correct one in THAT FIELD AND NOWHERE ELSE (`2 2 2 0` against `0 2 2 0`),
so a driver built from the lists and the events alone would have passed the
exact waste this source was written to remove. The two crashes are failures
too, just cruder ones: the driver indexes the event and woken lists, and a
missing entry takes the probe out rather than printing a wrong line.

  --- and case 5, closing every bridge: `<p10> <p11> <p12> <fired>` ---

  close `2`   PORT 10 IS CLOSED. A bridge, open, so it is the one port this
              function exists to act on. `P_CLOSED` is 2.
  close `1`   **PORT 11 IS STILL OPEN**, and this is the field that refuses an
              implementation which closes the list rather than the bridges in
              it. It is a CHANNEL: both its ends live in this heap, nobody
              outside is waiting to be told, and closing it here would end a
              conversation between two green threads that the sandbox shutting
              its front door has no business ending. `crosses-a-heap` is the
              whole test, and only this field checks that it is asked.
  close `2`   PORT 12 WAS ALREADY CLOSED and is unchanged -- which on its own
              proves nothing, since 2 is what closing it again would also
              leave. The next field is what makes it mean something.
  close `10`  **THE SIDE EFFECTS FIRED FOR 10 AND FOR NOTHING ELSE.** Written
              as the ids in order, not as a count, because the question is
              WHICH: an implementation missing the `P_CLOSED` test would read
              `10,12` here and would have told the host that port 12 was
              closed and released a SECOND time. One release per retain is
              what makes the host's number a count of holders rather than of
              arrivals, so a duplicate is not a tidiness problem -- it makes
              the host let go of a port somebody still holds. Id 13 is in the
              list with no row at all, so a missing nil check prints nothing
              here at all rather than a wrong value.
  close `1`   A NIL SCHEDULER IS NOT AN ERROR. Reaching the line is the
              assertion, the same reading `park-on-port` takes: the runtime
              can be torn down before a scheduler was ever built.

  --- and cases 7-10, what follows from one end closing:
      `<events> <held> <woken> <peer>` ---

  close `3:30,5:30`  CLOSED THEN RELEASE, in that order, both naming the port's
                     own id. Written as `kind:id` pairs rather than counted,
                     because the ORDER is part of the contract and a count
                     cannot see it: swapping the two reads `5:30,3:30` here.
  close `0`          THE ID HAS LEFT THE HELD LIST. That is what stops the
                     collector's sweep sending a SECOND release for a port the
                     program has already let go, and one release per retain is
                     what makes the host's number a count of holders. (Where
                     `forget-bridge` sits BETWEEN the two events is not
                     observable and is not asserted -- it pushes nothing, and
                     no host can see the interleaving. Saying so beats implying
                     a field covers it.)
  close `30,31`      BOTH ENDS WOKEN, this one and the peer.
  close `4`          THE PEER IS HALF-CLOSED, NOT CLOSED. It may still drain
                     what is already in its buffer and only then read
                     end-of-stream; closing it here would throw away messages
                     that were legitimately sent.

  close `-`          **A CHANNEL SAYS NOTHING TO THE HOST.** Both its ends live
                     in this heap, so there is nobody outside to tell. This is
                     the field that refuses an implementation which events
                     unconditionally -- it reads `3:40,5:40` without the
                     `crosses-a-heap` test, and every other field in that group
                     is unchanged.
  close `0 40,41 4`  ... and everything that does NOT depend on the kind still
                     happens: both ends woken, peer half-closed.

  close `3:50,5:50 0 50 2`
                     **A PEER ALREADY CLOSED IS LEFT ENTIRELY ALONE.** Not moved
                     to HALF -- a tidy close outranks a half, the same reading
                     `reap-all` takes when it refuses to orphan a closed peer --
                     and NOT WOKEN, because there is nothing new for it to
                     learn. Both live in one `if`, and the woken list is the
                     only field that can tell whether the test guards the wake
                     as well as the write: dropping the test reads
                     `50,51 4` here.
  close `3:60,5:60 0 60 -`
                     NO PEER AT ALL. The bridge half still happens in full;
                     reaching the line is the assertion for the nil check.

